Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Memory-context chunks as Sublet lifetimes
Upstream: postgresql 17.0 and 17.5
Build-Variants: Sublet replay and PGSU_NESTED=sublet
Depends-On: patches 0001 and 0002 of the memory-context component
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Every chunk a memory context hands out is a child lifetime (CDERIVE) of
its block's generation, bounded to the chunk header and MAXALIGN(size).
pfree and repalloc end it (CREVOKE), so every copy of the caller's
pointer dies with it. Allocator metadata and libc calls use the block's
malloc reference. The chunk header keeps that reference, so pfree needs
nothing but the caller's pointer.

A block derives its generation on first use. A reset that keeps the
block revokes the generation, which ends every chunk of the block, and
the next allocation derives a new one. Reset therefore takes no table
slot and cannot fail. Freeing a block ends its malloc lifetime and every
chunk below it.

CDERIVE returns an untagged 0 when the lifetime table has no free slot
and cannot grow. The managers then give the chunk back through their own
free path (Bump rewinds its cursor) and report out of memory the way
they do for a failed malloc. Below 4 KiB the bounds are exact. Above,
the bounds format rounds both edges to a granule, and the managers keep
their placement: the child then also covers the granule slack around
the chunk, inside its block's malloc object.

Every realloc creates the new chunk before it ends the old one. The
in-place paths issue the new child, then revoke the old. The moving
paths allocate and copy first. An AllocSet single-chunk block uses
malloc, issue, copy and free instead of libc realloc, which would end
the old block before the new chunk exists. A failed realloc therefore
leaves the old chunk live and unchanged.

The bounds include the chunk header. A live caller can therefore load
its block's malloc reference from the header. This patch does not
isolate allocator metadata from a live caller.

